For those unfamiliar with some of the platforms which this interpreter
supports, some brief descriptions follow.

The Acorn Archimedes range consists of RISC based personal computers
which have been manufactured since 1987 by Acorn computers in the UK.
Although Acorn dominate the UK education market, the machines have
attracted a reasonable following in the hobbyist market not least
because of the machine's performance and approachable assembly
language.  The current versions of the range are Acorn's RiscPC's
which feature a multiple, upgradable CPU architecture supporting both
the 486 and ARM6/ARM7/StrongARM in a single machine.

The Psion Series3 range consists of a family of pocket organisers
based around derivatives of the 8086 family but running a custom
pre-emptive multitasking operating system.  Unlike many of its
competitors, the Psion aims to be a real computer and effective
organiser rather than a tiny DOS compatible.  (The UK designed and
manufactured Series3 is also sold under licence as the Acorn
Pocketbook into the education market, with the Pocketbook II
corresponding the the enhanced Series3A.)

X-Windows is the dominant GUI on Unix workstations; applications talk
to the display using network protocols allowing a single display to
show the output from programs running on many systems.

(The author pleads guilty to a fondness for innovative and technically
excellent UK-designed products over mainstream PCs, especially when
computing for pleasure.)
